TI  - NSF-PINN: Physics-informed source-filter decomposition with sharpness-aware minimization for generalizable audio deepfake detection
PY  - 2026
T2  - Computer Vision and Image Understanding
VL  - 271
C7  - 104875
DO  - 10.1016/j.cviu.2026.104875
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045233277?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Computer Science and Information Technology, Central University of Jammu, Samba, 181143, India
AB  - The unprecedented audio quality from current neural speech synthesis and voice conversion systems highlights the urgent necessity for robust deepfake detection methods. Prior detection methods fail to generalize to unseen spoofing conditions due to two underlying causes: reliance on artifact-specific cues from specific synthesis pipelines and convergence to sharp minima, where performance degrades under distribution shift. In this work, we introduce NSF-PINN (Neural Source-Filter Physics-Informed Neural Network), a principled architecture that explicitly tackles both issues. Here, physics-informed refers to the incorporation of source-filter principles from speech production theory, encoding domain knowledge about natural speech generation directly into the learning process. NSF-PINN disentangles glottal excitation and vocal tract resonance from WavLM-Base+ latent representations via a dedicated source-filter decomposition branch guided by three physics-based objectives: reconstruction consistency, temporal smoothness, and source-filter orthogonality. These losses penalize physically unrealizable speech patterns present in synthesized samples but absent in real speech. Concurrently, Sharpness-Aware Minimization (SAM) guides optimization toward a flat loss landscape, yielding parameter settings that transfer across synthesis conditions rather than memorizing training artifacts. This introduces two-level regularization, physics priors at the feature level and SAM at the parameter level. Evaluated on ASVspoof 2019 LA, ASVspoof 2021 DF, In-the-Wild, and FakeAVCeleb, NSF-PINN achieves 4.49%, 12.38%, 19.4%, and 21.8% EER, respectively. While larger SSL-based systems retain an advantage on controlled benchmarks, NSF-PINN substantially outperforms all raw-waveform baselines and narrows the performance gap on challenging cross-domain evaluations. Ablation studies confirm the complementary architectural benefits, yielding a 44.9% EER reduction relative to the WavLM baseline. © 2026 Elsevier Inc.
