UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105023706616?origin=resultslist
AD  - College of Mechanical and Vehicle Engineering, Hunan University, Changsha, 410073, China
AB  - In recent years, domain generalization methods have gradually emerged, aiming to mitigate the impact of domain shifts and enhance the model's generalization ability under unseen working conditions. However, domain generalization-based fault diagnosis methods face challenges due to the difficulty of obtaining sufficient high-quality labeled data, and improving the generalization capability of the models remains an urgent issue. To address these challenges, a two-stage semi-supervised domain generalization network is proposed, which integrates pseudo-label generation with domain generalization learning, addressing the problem of label scarcity while enhancing the generalization capability of models in industrial fault diagnosis. In the pseudo-labeling stage, domain adaptation strategies are employed to assign pseudo-labels to unlabeled source domain samples, addressing the issue of limited labeled data. In the generalization stage, discriminative-enhanced domain-invariant feature mixup and large margin loss are used to improve the diversity of features and reduce synthetic noise. Sharpness-aware minimization gradient matching is applied to achieve more robust optimization and reduce the risk of overfitting. Additionally, a domain label smoothing strategy is introduced to reduce over-classification by the domain discriminator. Experimental results show that the proposed model achieves higher diagnostic accuracy and greater stability compared to existing state-of-the-art semi-supervised domain generalization methods on two bearing datasets. Copyright © 2025. Published by Elsevier Ltd.
