AB  - Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs) have gained attention for their biological plausibility and energy efficiency. While many techniques have been proposed to improve their generalization, explicitly optimizing for flat minima remains largely unexplored. Sharpness-Aware Minimization (SAM) enhances generalization in Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs) by promoting flat minima in weight space, but directly applying SAM to SNNs may overlook spike-driven dynamics that are critical to their behavior. In this work, we revisit flatness in SNNs and propose Threshold-SAM, a variant of SAM that explicitly encourages flat minima along the neuronal threshold dimension. Theoretically, we show that Threshold-SAM and weight-space SAM induce distinct implicit regularization effects, and experimental results further verify that they yield different out-of-distribution generalization improvements. Moreover, Threshold-SAM achieves a top-1 accuracy of 85.69% on ImageNet-1K and improves performance under reduced time steps and threshold perturbations. These results highlight the importance of threshold-space flatness as a complementary approach to enhancing SNN generalization by modulating spike-driven dynamics. The code of our experiments is available at https://github.com/MinJoker/Threshold-SAM. © 2026 IEEE.
