AB  - THIS addresses errors in sentence of Section III, which is missing a reference [1]. The error is in the first to [11]. The error is in Section III-A. The supplemented content will better reflect the unique characteristics of sensor data while maintaining sensor-specific innovations. Below Are the Modifications: Given any r 0, 0 α 1, sample size m - N+, and parameter dimension k = dim(h), the bound expressed with at least 1 − α, for a training set S independently and identically sampled from distribution T , is:(Formula presented). The supplementary content includes the literature citations [26], [46], [47], PAC-Bayesian-based “sharpness” concept elaboration, and complete mathematical derivations for parameter selection. This enhancement provides theoretical foundations and transforms abstract concepts into a practical framework with academic rigor. Optimizing the aforementioned generalization bound helps in finding model parameters that can achieve a flat minimum. By doing so, one can select an appropriate value of r such that within this radius, the model remains robust under parameter perturbations, meaning that within this range, the model’s loss does not exhibit significant fluctuations due to minor variations in parameters. Hence, there exists an optimal r. The error is in (2), (3), (5), and (6). 1) In (2), as the sample size increases, the log(m/α) term becomes negligible, so using ≈ and omitting this term simply the expression while improving computational efficiency and numerical stability for long-tail sensor data processing. 2) In (3), there is a mathematical notation error: the constraint ∥µ∥2≤ √kr has an incorrect radical scope. 3) In (5), the term (∥h∥2 2 /2 ∥∇h LS (h)∥2 2) erroneously contained squares in both the numerator and denominator. 4) The original formula (6) omitted some relevant contents, which might lead to misunderstanding. Below Are the Modifications: (Formula presented) Substituting (3) into (2) and assuming r ≪ ∥h∥2 such that we can ignore log(m/α), we can obtain an approximate value for r*(Formula presented) The term max ∥µ∥2≤(kr)1/2* LS (h + µ) represents the sharpness of the loss function LS at the point h, while (1/2(m −1)1/2) · (∥h∥2 /r*) is the L2 regularization. The error is in Section III-B. Since the formulas in Section III-A have been modified, the corresponding formulas in Section III-B also need to be revised. Below Are the Modifications: In this section, we elaborate on how to implement the decoupled training first stage, i.e., how to optimize deep learning models by leveraging class-specific generalization bounds to enhance generalization performance on long-tail distribution data. Due to the pronounced difference in label distribution between the training and testing sets, the crucial assumption that the training data S are independently and identically distributed (i.i.d.) from the target distribution T is not valid. To address this issue, we instead utilize the classconditional distributions ps(x|y) and pt(x|y). Additionally, we decompose the total loss LT (h) into the sum of losses for each class, allowing for the independent treatment of loss and generalization bounds for each class. This helps reduce overfitting and neglect of minority classes. Class-specific generalization bound(Formula presented) where theperturbation constraint term is ∥µ∥2 ≤ (k)1/2r∗, which means considering the maximum loss when the model parameters h are perturbed by α. Additionally, the classspecific generalization bound Lˆ Tj (h) is positively correlated with the class gradient norm ∇h LSj (h) 2. This implies that the model’s performance on samples with higher loss (difficult to classify) directly influences the generalization capability of the class, effectively enforcing hard example mining. Feature radius(Formula presented) We have supplemented relevant academic references within the paragraph described after (11) and (12). The error is in Section III-C. Since the formulas in Section III-A have been modified, the corresponding formulas in the pseudocode also need to be revised. The content in other locations of the table remains unchanged; only modify the content within the table. (Formula presented) In this section, we introduce a category-specific flattening optimization algorithm specifically designed for activity of daily living recognition (ADLR) applications [11]. The reason for this modification is that the previous discussion of sensor technology was too superficial. We now deepen it by introducing time-series parameters, multivariate sensor representations, and spatiotemporal dimensions, connecting theory directly to ADLR applications and sensor data processing needs. The Corresponding Modifications: In sensor-driven long-tailed activity recognition tasks, assume the training set S contains m time-series data points independently sampled from distribution T(with time steps t and sensor channels c), input data matrices X ∈ Rt×c representing multivariate time series from multiple sensor channels, and model parameters h ∈ Rc×d. For any perturbation strength r > 0, confidence level 0 < α < 1, and total spatiotemporal dimensions k = c.t, the generalization error bound holds with probability at least 1 − α To make our sharpness term explicit, (2) presents an explicit decomposition of the sharpness term. The key value of this decomposition lies in rendering the concept of “sharpness” quantifiable and operationalizable [26]. Inspired by the literature [46], [47], our theoretical analysis [see (1)] ensures a nonvacuous generalization bound, addressing the issue of traditional PAC-Bayesian bounds being overly loose [11]. Building upon this solid theoretical foundation and the guarantee of nonvacuous bounds, we can now leverage the decomposed bound for practical optimization. Optimizing the aforementioned generalization bound helps in finding model parameters that can achieve a flat minimum. By doing so, one can select an appropriate value of r such that within this radius, the model remains robust under parameter perturbations, meaning that within this range, the model’s loss does not exhibit significant fluctuations due to minor variations in parameters. Hence, there exists an optimal r∗. Due to the addition of a new equation in the preceding text, all subsequent equation numbers have been accordingly incremented by one to maintain numbering continuity and consistency. 1) The modification simplifies (3) by removing the negligible log(m/α) term and replacing the equality with an approximation symbol “≈” to improve computational efficiency for long-tail distribution problems. 2) The proper mathematical expression should be ∥µ∥2 ≤ (k)1/2r, where the square root applies only to parameter k, not to the entire kr product. 3) We have corrected (6) by removing the redundant term. 4) To help readers more clearly understand (7), we have provided an additional explanation of its derivation process. The Corresponding Modifications : (Formula Praesented) Substituting (4) into (3), we can obtain an approximate value for r* (Formula Praesented) Formula (7) can be obtained by substituting (4) and (6) into (1) (Formula Praesented) The Corresponding Modifications: This section delineates a novel decoupled training first-stage methodology specifically engineered to address the long-tail distribution phenomenon in sensor-based ADLR systems. Within ADLR applications, a significant data imbalance exists wherein high-frequency activities are represented by abundant training samples, whereas critical but low-frequency activities suffer from limited data representation. This imbalance invariably results in suboptimal model performance for these crucial minority classes, potentially compromising the system’s efficacy in monitoring essential health-related behaviors. Our principal methodological innovation, inspired by [11], lies in the discrete treatment of individual activity categories through conditional probability distributions p(x|y) rather than conventional joint distributions, enabling more effective modeling of class-specific characteristics. According to (7), it can be obtained that the class-specific generalization bound L̂Tj (H) ≈ √2 (∥h∥2 ∗ ∥∇hLS (h)∥2)12 k41 (m j − 1)− 14 (9) where the formula reflects the performance limit for rare activities when sensor data exhibit heterogeneous noise patterns. Additionally, the activity-specific generalization capability is inversely related to the available training samples m j for each activity class. This implies that the model’s recognition accuracy on infrequent activities (such as medication-taking) directly impacts the overall system reliability, effectively prioritizing critical but data-scarce daily living activities. (Formula Praesented) . Formulas (12) and (13) update the parameter h using a learning rate. The update algorithm mentioned above only involves the first-order gradients, which leads to high computational efficiency [26]. Additionally, as h approaches the optimal solution h∗, the gradients of each class ∇hL̂Tj (h)|h∗ remain far from zero, which helps bypass the need for higher order terms. (Formula presented). © 2001-2012 IEEE.
