AD  - College of Mechanical and Electrical Engineering, Beijing Information Science & Technology University, Beijing, 100192, China
AB  - Deep Learning-based intelligent fault diagnosis has achieved widespread application, yet it still hinges on large-scale datasets with high-quality labels. In practical industrial settings, unavoidable label noise can lead to severe overfitting and severely degrade diagnostic performance. In this work, by integrating a time–frequency network (TFN) with a frequency-aware adaptive (FAA) strategy and extended invariant risk minimization (EIRM), we propose a robust diagnosis framework named TFN-FAA-EIRM. First, an EIRM penalty is incorporated into the loss function to steer the optimization toward flat minima and to improve robustness against noise-induced parameter perturbations. Second, we build a FAA strategy on top of a physics-inspired TFN. This strategy uses gradient momentum to assign adaptive regularization to different frequency channels. Specifically, it imposes stronger constraints on suspected noise-dominated bands while applying relatively lower penalties to potentially fault-relevant ones. Extensive experiments on three rotating machinery fault datasets with symmetric and asymmetric label noise show that TFN-FAA-EIRM substantially improves robustness. Averaged over the three datasets, it improves test accuracy from about 27% to 68% under 80% symmetric noise and from about 57% to 85% under 45% asymmetric noise. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the IOP-Standard License.
