AD  - Center for Research and Advanced Studies, Km. 5.5 Carretera Ciudad Victoria a Soto La Marina, Parque TECNOTAM, Tamaulipas, Ciudad Victoria, 87138, Mexico
AB  - This work develops a unified probabilistic framework in which the training dynamics of both classical and quantum neural networks are governed by limit shapes: deterministic macroscopic profiles that arise from the collective averaging of microscopic stochastic fluctuations. Using the Law of Large Numbers, the Central Limit Theorem, and the Large Deviation Principle, we show that the parameter trajectories generated by stochastic gradient descent concentrate exponentially around the minimizer of an explicit rate functional. This minimizer is the classical limit shape, and fluctuations around it are Gaussian with covariance determined by the Hessian of the loss. We extend this theory to quantum neural networks, where variational unitary circuits define trajectories in Hilbert space. In the quantum setting, the same entropy–energy balance governs convergence: the von Neumann entropy replaces the Shannon entropy, and a quantum large deviation principle yields concentration around a deterministic density operator. Fluctuations are described by a quantum Gaussian limit governed by the quantum Fisher information metric. Our results include proofs of existence and uniqueness of limit shapes for both classical and quantum models, as well as numerical demonstrations in shallow ReLU networks and small quantum circuits. These experiments confirm the theoretical predictions: entropy peaks coincide with entry into flat minima, parameter distributions converge to Gaussian envelopes, and deviations are exponentially suppressed. By placing learning dynamics within the statistical–mechanical framework of free energy minimization, this work provides a rigorous foundation for entropy-aware training. It explains why overparameterized models exhibit robustness and generalization, and it motivates entropy-regularized optimization protocols that accelerate convergence and enhance noise resilience in classical and quantum regimes. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
