AB  - The widely used Adam optimizer usually converges towards sharp minima with poorer generalization compared to the more straightforward SGD. While this is often attributed to the geometry of loss landscapes, we instead posit that it stems from the heuristic Exponential Moving Average (EMA) used by Adam for moment estimation. As a remedy, we introduce Kalman-Adam, a fundamentally novel optimizer that discards the traditional EMA. Instead, it pioneers a principled Bayesian framework, modeling moment estimation as a state-space problem where two independent Kalman filters optimally estimate moments from noisy mini-batch gradients. We conjecture that this results in a more reliable update signal, driving optimization towards superior, flatter minima. Extensive evaluation, using ResNet-18 and ResNet-50 architectures, on nine benchmarks including CIFAR, Tiny ImageNet, and the challenging fine-grained visual classification (FGVC) datasets shows that Kalman-Adam surpasses the current industry standard of AdamW in 7 out of 9 tasks. These include remarkable accuracy gains on FGVC-Aircraft (+1.65% over AdamW) and a new state-of-the-art on Flowers-102 (+3.17%), even outperforming SGD. Unexpectedly, memory profiling reveals that Kalman-Adam is remarkably efficient, reducing peak memory consumption by approximately 14% compared to SGD on high-resolution datasets. Supporting this, loss landscape analysis confirms that Kalman-Adam converges to significantly flatter minima, providing a mechanistic explanation for its improved generalization. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
