AB  - With the rapid proliferation of Internet-of-Things (IoT) and smart cyber - physical systems, the privacy preserving, trusted, and real time intrusion detection has experienced an extreme surge of demand. Traditional centralized deep learning methods have severe imperfections on data privacy, regulation, and communication overheads; and lightweight baselines such as CNNibased and LSTMbased are prone to overfit in heterogenous IoT environment. Inspired by the aforementioned issues, this paper proposes BC-FedHVAE-GAT - a high-performance Blockchain-Enhanced FedHVGA-GAT-GRU model with Sharpness-Aware Minimization (SAM) for learning stability. The model integrates hierarchical VAE-based latent encoding, attention mechanism guided graph learning and GRU module for temporal modeling that enables joint representation learning over heterogeneous network flows while also preserving device-level privacy via federated training. Alternatively, blockchain allows for extracting irreducible audit trails and distributed compliance control. The experiments were conducted on the TON-IoT network dataset. This proposed HybridV2 (SAM) model has a convergence stability and the convergence process monotonically converges to the lowest loss without gradient explosion. In our experiments, we compare the Inner Hybrid model with state-of-the-art baselines on all the models which in boldface are comparisons under their own training strategy, the accuracy of Inner Hybrid model is 88.1 %, compared to 87.1% and 86.7% of CNN and LSTM (best viewed in color). Furthermore, HybridV2 has much smoother learning curves, stronger overfitting control and better late-epoch accuracy improvement compared to Hybrid so that it is more suitable for smart-governance regimes in realistic environments of real-time and distributed systems. The results have demonstrated the ability of BC-FedHVAE-GAT to significantly enhance detection capability with no compromise on privacy and regulatory transparency, and computational scalability issues (performance trade-offs), making it a viable candidate for IoT security solutions in future governance frameworks. © 2026 IEEE.
