AB  - While low-precision formats (e.g., BF16) reduce memory usage, standard training still requires high-precision (FP32) master weights to mitigate the “swamping” phenomenon, where small updates are numerically ignored (θ + δ = θ). This redundancy halves the potential memory savings. In this paper, we propose a Relative ULP Noise Injection mechanism to achieve fully low-precision training without master weights or high-precision optimizer states. Instead of accumulating residuals, our method conditionally amplifies swamped updates to ±1 Unit in Last Place (ULP) based on their relative significance. This creates a geometric “Noise Ball” effect that prevents stalling and implicitly regularizes the model towards flatter minima. Experiments on CNNs (ResNet18) and Transformers (T5) demonstrate that our method matches or exceeds high-precision (FP32) baselines. Furthermore, it reduces memory consumption by up to 50% and achieves computational efficiency comparable to standard half-precision training, making large-scale training feasible on consumer-grade hardware. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
