AB  - The current paper suggests a training methodology based on Dynamic Feature Regularization (DFR), which can be used to enhance the level of generalization in deep models by dynamically regulating feature representations internally. DRF monitors feature allow loss observes instead of either using a constant weight or loss decay rate, or removal rate, and changes the strength of regularization dynamically and per feature. The approach promotes small, discriminative, and low-redundancy embeddings and maintains variability of the tasks at hand. DFR, instantiate DFR as a plug and play module, which can be incorporated into existing convolutional and transformer architectures with the smallest number of modifications to the optimization pipeline. Benchmark vision and text classification experimentation specify that DFR tends to decrease overfitting, reduce the performance disparity between the train and testing phases, and increasing calibration findings in case of a shift in the distribution as compared to standard L2, dropout, and sharpness-sensitive training. The studies of ablation emphasize feature-adaptive scheduling is vital and suggest that DFR is the antithesis of data augmentation. In general, the methodology provides a feasible path to stronger and more universally applicable deep models. The code and configuration templates are given to enable repeats of the results.  © 2026 IEEE.
