AD  - School of Electrical Engineering, Xinjiang University, Xinjiang, China
AB  - Ultra-short-term wind-farm power forecasting is crucial for secure system operation and risk-aware dispatch. However, accuracy and reliability can be degraded by time-varying wake interactions, strong non-stationarity, and distribution shifts during extreme conditions. This paper proposes PDWF-CQR, a physics-driven probabilistic forecasting framework that couples dynamic wake modeling with step-dependent conformalized quantile regression (CQR) calibration. Turbine-level power series are first decomposed using complete ensemble empirical mode decomposition with adaptive noise (CEEMDAN) to obtain multi-scale components, and cross-turbine order alignment is applied to form scale-consistent inputs. We then construct a physics-driven, time-varying directed wake graph by mapping wake-induced velocity deficits to edge weights under wind-direction gating and interaction-range truncation, enabling explicit modeling of evolving wake coupling. A graph convolutional network (GCN) spatial encoder and a Transformer–BiLSTM temporal module are combined to learn spatio-temporal representations. For probabilistic forecasting, multi-quantile regression is trained with a power-curve-consistency regularization to suppress non-physical oscillations of interval endpoints, and step-dependent CQR further recalibrates multi-horizon prediction intervals to achieve near-nominal empirical coverage. Case studies on two operational wind farms show that PDWF-CQR improves point-forecasting accuracy and produces well-calibrated prediction intervals with a favorable coverage–sharpness trade-off, remaining robust under high-turbulence and rapid-ramping scenarios. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
