AB  - Domain generalization methods aim to enhance the adaptability of mechanical fault diagnosis models under unseen working conditions. Nevertheless, complex and varying industrial operating conditions still challenge generalization performance. Although large scale pre-trained models have demonstrated generalization capabilities, their application to fault diagnosis remains limited. To address this issue, this paper proposes a sharpness aware flattening for domain generalization with pre-trained vision transformer (SDGViT) method in machinery fault diagnosis. First, a dynamically mapped low-rank (DMLR) adapter with few parameters is designed, which introduces a learnable scaling vector to enhance the model’s expressive flexibility, enabling it to mine representative features from diagnostic data while preserving the feature extraction capability of the pre-trained vision transformer. Second, the proposed sharpness aware flattening (SAF) method enhances model generalization by flattening the loss landscape. Specifically, a conditional circular Gaussian smoothing (CCGS) strategy is proposed for adaptively smoothing the long-range path according to local loss landscape conditions, thereby facilitating reaching a better global optimum. Meanwhile, a variable sharpness aware minimization (VSAM) method is developed for short-range loss landscape flattening, which simultaneously minimizes the loss value and sharpness to induce the convergence of a model at a low loss value to the desired flat zone. Experiments on fault diagnosis cases validate the superiority of SDGViT compared with other domain generalization methods, achieving an average test accuracy of 98.74% and demonstrating a favorable generalization ability in machinery fault diagnosis. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
