AB  - It is important that skin cancer is detected early and also accurately to increase patient survival. Here, we present our proposed model that is the first hybrid deep learning architecture, DermalNetTransformer, to perform multiple tasks in skin lesion analysis, including classification, segmentation, and malignancy risk regression, using dermoscopic images as the input. This model is founded on a CNN-based encoder (ResNet50) that helps to extract local texture features at a given location and Transformer-based attention module that helps to extract the global contextual dependencies to comprehend the features on a global scale. The model was also trained on multi-task task that consisted of focal loss (classification), boundary-aware Dice loss (segmentation), and mean squared error (risk regression) with optimization using Sharpness-Aware Minimization (SAM) and AdamW with a cosine learning schedule. A full test on a selected set of 8200 images achieved a Macro AUROC of 0.969, Balanced Accuracy of 0.923 and Dice score of 0.852 and a high cross-validation reliability. A StudentNet that had been compressed with knowledge achieved the AUROC of 0.939 a fraction of the model size, 1.8 times faster inference and could be run in a mobile or embedded platform. Clinical transparency was ensured by using visual explainability using Grad-CAM and segmentation overlays. The suggested DermalNetTransformer shows that the hybrid structure of local and global feature learning assisted by boundary-guided segmentation can significantly increase the quality of automated skin cancer diagnosis in addition to transparency and computational efficiency.  © 2025 IEEE.
