AB  - Cancer is a life-threatening disease, specifically leukemia causes early death. Now a days, children under 5 years old are getting affected by Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL), because of its late identification. If early classification is possible, it becomes easier to take the necessary steps for effective treatment. To improve the classification of ALL in pediatric patients, this study makes use of the EfficientNetV2-B3. Besides, three optimizers are compared in the study: ADAM, SGD and RMSprop. The results indicate that the model's performance is impacted by the optimizer selection. SGD gave the best results in the experiment. It showed an accuracy of 92.30%, precision of 92%, recall of 96%, F1-score of 94%, and MCC of 0.8265. On the other hand, RMSprop performed the worst. It had an accuracy of 85.53% and precision of 73.0%, leading to more false positives. ADAM had the highest recall at 99.0%. Its lower precision of 87.0% and MCC of 0.7791 indicate possible overfitting and lower generalization. These results validate that non-adaptive optimizers such as SGD can achieve better generalization and flatter minima in medical image classification tasks when properly tuned. © 2026 IEEE.
