AB  - Electric-vehicle energy management benefits from uncertainty-Aware multi-horizon forecasts of battery state of charge (SoC), especially near regenerative-braking events. We studied a conformalized LSTM forecaster on a three-wheeled EV platform and report calibrated prediction intervals for short and long horizons under distribution shift. Experiments retained the original pipeline with standardization fit on the full dataset and conformal margins fixed from clean calibration. Two calibration schemes were tested, Šidák per-horizon and uniform max-residual, for family-wise coverage across horizons. On clean data, BiLSTM attains near-nominal marginal and family-wise coverage with competitive sharpness, while UniLSTM is narrower yet often under-covers at long horizons. We complemented these results with a noise-robust evaluation that perturbs standardized test inputs using Gaussian noise, constant bias, and 8-bit quantization. Family-wise coverage and weighted interval score (WIS) are summarized across feature modes. In the long-horizon setting, adding lags and using BiLSTM yields family-wise PICP about 1,000 with moderate widths, whereas lean UniLSTM variants under-cover. Reliability curves corroborated these findings. We reused an existing dataset from the same platform, with BMS-reported SoC as labels. We recommend train-split scaling for deployment and future ablations. Overall, a conformalized LSTM with appropriate calibration provides reliable uncertainty for safety-critical SoC forecasting on lightweight EVs.)  © 2025 IEEE.
